The system microservices topology divides key responsibilities across dedicated operational services:An API Gateway and Authentication Service handles inbound traffic, enforces tenant-level rate-limiting tiers, manages enterprise Single Sign-On (SSO), and governs OAuth 2.0 token lifecycles. The gateway decrypts tenant credentials, validates incoming session signatures, and routes requests down to underlying microservices.The Scheduling and Queue Management Subsystem operates as a high-precision distributed task engine built on Redis and BullMQ or Apache Kafka. It maintains cron triggers and timestamped message queues that dispatch publishing jobs to worker pools with sub-second accuracy.The Media Processing and Transcoding Engine handles uploaded assets asynchronously. It inspects binary files, converts images to standardized color profiles, transcodes video streams using FFmpeg, generates UI thumbnails, and uploads optimized media to Amazon S3 backed by CloudFront CDN distribution.The Unified Publishing Worker Pipeline executes social platform API calls. It contains platform-specific driver modules that manage multi-step request flows, inspect HTTP status responses, handle rate limits via backoff algorithms, and process partial success states.The Analytics Ingestion and Data Warehouse Subsystem processes continuous streams of engagement metrics, ad metrics, and webhook updates. Built on an event streaming architecture using Apache Kafka and ClickHouse or TimescaleDB, it facilitates rapid aggregation queries across massive, multi-tenant time-series datasets.The MCP AI Gateway exposes platform REST APIs as structured Model Context Protocol tools. This protocol translates conversational requests from external AI agents into authenticated API operations, permitting automated reporting and scheduling.Media processing requires strict validation against target network specifications prior to calling external APIs. Uploaded images are re-encoded to sRGB color spaces to eliminate color profile shifts across devices. Images are scaled to conform with aspect ratio boundaries, such as 4: 5 to 1.91: 1 for standard Instagram feed posts or 9: 16 for visual stories, ensuring pixel dimensions fall within acceptable thresholds (minimum 320px width up to 1920px horizontal). Video files are transcoded into H.264/AAC MP4 or MOV formats. The engine verifies frame rates (23.976 to 60 fps), bitrates, file sizes (up to 1 GB for short-form video reels, or up to 300 MB for carousel video slides), and vertical 9: 16 aspect ratios to prevent processing failures on external platform servers.Queue execution and webhook processing prioritize fault isolation and idempotency. Every scheduled job carries an immutable UUID that prevents duplicate publication during network retries. Rate limiters maintain sliding-window execution logs per connected social account, throttling worker threads when approaching API quotas. Multi-platform publishing requests process platform targets independently. If a broadcast to Instagram, LinkedIn, and X succeeds on two networks but fails on Instagram due to an expired token, the worker logs a partial success state (INSTAGRAM: FAILED, LINKEDIN: SUCCESS, X: SUCCESS), keeping the overall workflow transparent. Inbound webhooks pass through an verification engine that validates cryptographic signature headers (such as Meta's X-Hub-Signature-256) before placing payloads into processing queues.Deep-Dive Protocol Integration and API Engineering SpecificationsExecuting social media publishing requires navigating diverse platform protocols, token management pipelines, and strict rate-limiting constraints.OAuth 2.0 Security and Token Management EngineThe token management engine exchanges short-lived user tokens received during OAuth callbacks for long-lived access tokens, such as Meta's 60-day user tokens. Access tokens are encrypted at rest using AES-256-GCM. A background token maintenance worker polls the credential database, automatically refreshing tokens that approach expiration windows (typically within 15 days of expiration) to prevent authorization lapses. When platforms revoke tokens or report sub-error codes indicating password changes or security invalidations (e.g., Meta sub-errors 463 or 467), the system isolates the profile, transitions its status to NEEDS_REAUTHENTICATION, pauses its active queue, and dispatches alerts to workspace administrators.Instagram Graph API Container Publishing ProtocolsPublishing content to Instagram via the Graph API requires navigating an asynchronous container creation, status polling, and publication flow. The API mandates that target profiles be professional (Business or Creator) accounts connected to a Facebook Page.Single Item Flow:
